      "text": "We can use the parametric form of the line equation to find the $z$-coordinate of the point. The general form of a parametric line equation is\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}x_0 \\\\ y_0 \\\\ z_0\\end{pmatrix} + t\\begin{pmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{pmatrix},\\]where $\\begin{pmatrix}x_0 \\\\ y_0 \\\\ z_0\\end{pmatrix}$ is a point on the line and $\\begin{pmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{pmatrix}$ is the direction vector of the line.  We want to find a point on the line for $x=4$; [asy]\nunitsize(0.5 cm);\n\npair A, B, C;\n\nA = (2,2);\nB = (5,1);\nC = (4,3);\n\ndraw(A--B--(10,0)--B);\ndraw(C--B,dashed);\n\ndraw(B--(4,0),dashed);\n\ndot(\"$A$\", A, S);\ndot(\"$B$\", B, S);\ndot(\"$C$\", C, S);\n\nlabel(\"$x$\", (2,0), W);\nlabel(\"$y$\", (0,2), W);\nlabel(\"$z$\", (0,0), S);\n[/asy] we can see that $x_0 = 2, y_0 = 2,$ and $z_0 = 1.$  We also find the direction vector\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}5-2 \\\\ 1-2 \\\\ -2-1\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{pmatrix}.\\]So the equation for the line through $(2,2,1)$ and $(5,1,-2)$ is\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} + t\\begin{pmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{pmatrix}.\\]We want to find the $z$-coordinate of the point for $x = 4$.  Then we can plug in $x = 4$ into the parametric equation and solve for $t$:\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} + t\\begin{pmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 + t(-3)\\end{pmatrix}.\\]Setting $x = 4$, we get $4 = 2 + t(-3),$ so $t = -2/3$.\nPutting $t = -2/3$ into the parametric equation for the $z$-coordinate, we find that\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 + (-2/3)(-3)\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{pmatrix}.\\]Therefore, the $z$-coordinate of the point is $\\boxed{5}$. The answer is: 5",
