    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "$\\fbox{2}$.\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{L}$ (plus zero toward the z coordinate) is the line given by\n$[x, y, z] = [2,2,1]+{t}^{[3,-1,-3]}.$If $x = 4,$ then $t = {t_1} = {k_1}$ and we have $y = 2-{t_1}$ and $z = 1-{d_1}.$Thus, $4 = 2+{k_1}|z = {d_1}|.$Since $\\overrightarrow{d} = [3,-1,-3]|, {d_1} |{t_1} = (d_1)({t_1}) = ({d_1}){|{t_1}|^2} = 2$or ${d_1}|{t_1} = {\\frac{d}{2}}$.The only other way to find $t_1$ is $d_1|t_1| = -{\\frac{d}{2}}$This gives $t_1 = \\pm(\\frac{d}{2}) = \\pm(\\sqrt{|d|})$so, up to an invalid vector, the matrix for ${t_1}$ is ${k_1} = \\pm\\sqrt{|d|}$.\n\nFinally, $z = {d_1}-{d_1}|t_1| = 1+(-{\\frac{d}{2}}) = 2$Note: It is correct to find ${t_1} = 4 or -{\\frac{d}{2}}.$Indeed, then $z = 1 - \\frac{d}{2} = 2$.\n\nIf ${t_1} = |d| = 2-\\sqrt{|d|}$, then $z = {d_1}+\\frac{d}{2} = 2-\\frac{d}{2} = \\boxed{2}$It is correct to find ${t_1} = 2-\\frac{d}{2}$, though it is slightly lazy and you could have just done that and called it the halfway shot.",
